WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.914
Tristan da Cunha: the most remote inhabited island on Earth.

00:00:05.014 --> 00:00:09.162
It lies in the South Atlantic, about two thousand four hundred kilometres from St

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:11.876
Helena and two thousand eight hundred from Cape Town.

00:00:12.976 --> 00:00:15.881
About one hundred and thirty-two million years ago,

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:18.502
as Africa and South America began to separate,

00:00:18.502 --> 00:00:23.344
a plume of hot mantle erupted vast floods of lava in what are now Namibia and Brazil.

00:00:23.344 --> 00:00:26.933
As the Atlantic widened, the same plume built the Walvis Ridge.

00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:30.124
Tristan is the youngest island at the end of that track.

00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:35.699
The island is a single great cone rising to about two thousand and sixty metres.

00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:37.825
But most of the volcano is underwater:

00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:42.581
its flanks reach three thousand six hundred metres deep within twenty-five kilometres

00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:43.308
of the coast.

00:00:43.308 --> 00:00:45.434
Watch the sea drain away to reveal it.

00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:51.559
In 2020, Wolfram Geissler and colleagues published sonar maps of the seafloor around

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:52.276
the islands.

00:00:52.276 --> 00:00:55.626
They found four hundred and thirty young volcanic cones,

00:00:55.626 --> 00:00:57.839
and two previously unknown seamounts.

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:02.923
One of them, Isolde Seamount, was probably the source of a submarine eruption in 2004

00:01:02.923 --> 00:01:05.435
that washed pumice onto Tristan's beaches.
